  write: { h: 'Финальное задание. Напишите эссе или колонку (250–300 слов) на тему «Что мне открылось в английском языке и юморе» — используйте всё, что умеете: связки, инверсию, выделение, иронию, хотя бы одну эпиграмму собственного сочинения.', min: 230, use: ['never have I', 'what I', 'although', 'irony', 'paradox', 'it is a truth universally acknowledged'],
    model: 'It is a truth universally acknowledged that a learner in possession of a good textbook must be in want of more time. When I started this course, I believed that English was a set of rules: tenses, articles, irregular verbs. What I discovered, however, was something much more interesting — a way of thinking.\nNever had I noticed how much English depends on what is not said. Although the words "That\'s a brave idea" look like a compliment, a British colleague may be telling you, very politely, that your plan is a disaster. Not only did I have to learn grammar, but I also had to learn to read between the lines.\nThe sitcoms helped more than I expected. It was Chandler who taught me self-deprecation, Sheldon who showed me the danger of taking idioms literally, Leslie who proved that enthusiasm can be funny, and Dean who reminded me that you can joke even in the darkest moments. What these characters have in common is that their humour comes from who they are.\nThe greatest paradox of language learning, I think, is this: the more you know, the more you realise how much you still don\'t know. Yet that is exactly what makes it addictive.\nSo here is my own small epigram, written in honour of Oscar Wilde: I can understand every tense except the present — because by the time I have analysed it, it has already become the past.' },
